{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"TITLE:\"Growth rate inhibition metrics correct\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":6,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"27135972","source":"MED","pmid":"27135972","pmcid":"PMC4887336","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC4887336"]},"doi":"10.1038/nmeth.3853","title":"Growth rate inhibition metrics correct for confounders in measuring sensitivity to cancer drugs.","authorString":"Hafner M, Niepel M, Chung M, Sorger PK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hafner M","lastName":"Hafner","firstName":"Marc","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1337-7598"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"HMS LINCS Center Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Niepel M","lastName":"Niepel","firstName":"Mario","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1415-6295"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"HMS LINCS Center Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Chung M","lastName":"Chung","firstName":"Mirra","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"HMS LINCS Center Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sorger PK","lastName":"Sorger","firstName":"Peter K","initials":"PK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3364-1838"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"HMS LINCS Center Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3364-1838"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1337-7598"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1415-6295"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"13","journalIssueId":2413514,"dateOfPublication":"2016 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2016,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nat Methods","title":"Nature methods","essn":"1548-7105","issn":"1548-7091","nlmid":"101215604","isoabbreviation":"Nat Methods"},"printPublicationDate":"2016-06-01"},"pubYear":"2016","pageInfo":"521-527","abstractText":"Drug sensitivity and resistance are conventionally quantified by IC50 or Emax values, but these metrics are highly sensitive to the number of divisions taking place over the course of a response assay. The dependency of IC50 and Emax on division rate creates artefactual correlations between genotype and drug sensitivity, while obscuring valuable biological insights and interfering with biomarker discovery. We derive alternative small molecule drug-response metrics that are insensitive to division number. These are based on estimation of the magnitude of drug-induced growth rate inhibition (GR) using endpoint or time-course assays. We show that GR50 and GRmax are superior to conventional metrics for assessing the effects of small molecule drugs in dividing cells. Moreover, adopting GR metrics requires only modest changes in experimental protocols. We expect GR metrics to improve the study of cell signaling and growth using small molecules and biologics and to facilitate the discovery of drug-response biomarkers and the identification of drugs effective against specific patient-derived tumor cells.","affiliation":"HMS LINCS Center Laboratory of Systems Pharmacology, Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Swiss National Science Foundation","grantId":"147876","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"P50 GM107618","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"U54 HL127365","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line, Tumor"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Antineoplastic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biological Products","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Culture Techniques"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Count"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Inhibitory Concentration 50"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Proliferation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Dose-Response Relationship, Drug"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Resistance, Neoplasm"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Models, Theoretical"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Computer Simulation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Small Molecule Libraries","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Discovery","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"High-Throughput Screening Assays"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Antineoplastic Agents","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Biological Products","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Small Molecule Libraries","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subset